// Decision line 18, every spine pre-drains (PreDrain). Known open at
// inventory row C13 as a step the eager spine does not emit; the
// outcome below already holds today, by another road.
//
// `Hub` is a statement-position literal with no subscriptions of its
// own, so it takes the eager spine. Its run() publishes to its field
// `sub`, on the main pool. A pre-drain delivers the cell before the
// first teardown step; it is a delivery point, not a quiescence
// witness.
//
// Today the cell is delivered by run()'s own exit flush, which drains
// the bus for any body that may have published, before the eager
// spine starts (and a main locus's ingress quiesce ends in a drain of
// its own). No program here reaches the eager spine with a cell the
// exit flush did not see, so the outcome holds; the trace build (L2)
// shows the missing step, no `PreDrain` on the eager spine.
//
// Expected terminal outcome: `ev sub-heard` before `ev sub-dissolve`,
// judged by the harness: `delivered-before-teardown`.

type Ping { n: Int; }
topic Pings { payload: Ping; subject: "lifecycle.l18"; }

locus Sub {
    bus { subscribe Pings as on_ping; }
    fn on_ping(p: Ping) { println("ev sub-heard"); }
    dissolve() { println("ev sub-dissolve"); }
}

locus Hub {
    params { sub: Sub = Sub { }; }
    bus { publish Pings; }
    run() { Pings <- Ping { n: 1 }; }
}

fn main() { Hub { }; }
